that I hope you'll enjoy.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.029
- What's the single worst

00:03:58.029 --> 00:04:00.104
marketing decision that you can make?

00:04:00.104 --> 00:04:01.506
And I think we've all been there.

00:04:01.506 --> 00:04:04.665
It's that you start with a crappy product,

00:04:04.665 --> 00:04:08.146
but as marketers, this is
what we do day in and day out.

00:04:08.146 --> 00:04:12.080
Our job starts when the
product is finished,

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:14.009
but a growth actor says why?

00:04:14.009 --> 00:04:15.719
Why can't the product be improved?

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:17.886
Why does it have to start,

00:04:18.893 --> 00:04:20.497
why does it have to start
so late in the process?

00:04:20.497 --> 00:04:23.354
Why are we getting
handed this final product

00:04:23.354 --> 00:04:27.521
when market feedback would
incorporate into the product

00:04:28.872 --> 00:04:31.811
the elements that make it successful?

00:04:31.811 --> 00:04:34.906
So, when marketers stop
thinking of their job

00:04:34.906 --> 00:04:38.757
as to simply work on
what's already been done,

00:04:38.757 --> 00:04:40.134
but to answer these questions,

00:04:40.134 --> 00:04:42.212
who is the product for,
why would they use it,

00:04:42.212 --> 00:04:45.865
why do I use it, marketing
is baked into the product,

00:04:45.865 --> 00:04:47.723
so I love this quote from Paul Graham,

00:04:47.723 --> 00:04:49.118
the founder of Y Combinator.

00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:50.463
He says, "Make stuff people want."

00:04:50.463 --> 00:04:52.700
That's the best marketing
decision that you can make

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.498
and I think if you look at
a company like Instagram,

00:04:54.498 --> 00:04:55.932
you see this very clearly, right?

00:04:55.932 --> 00:04:59.731
Instagram launched as a Foursquare
competitor called Burbn.

00:04:59.731 --> 00:05:01.929
It was a geo-location service

00:05:01.929 --> 00:05:04.450
and it had a feature that allowed people

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:08.304
to upload photos and put filters on them.

00:05:08.304 --> 00:05:12.471
It got a little bit of traction,
but nothing truly profound

00:05:13.539 --> 00:05:15.392
and so they decided they're going to

00:05:15.392 --> 00:05:17.007
zoom in on this single feature.

00:05:17.007 --> 00:05:20.286
They're going to throw everything
that they've done out so far

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.549
and they're going to rebrand as Instagram.

00:05:22.549 --> 00:05:23.615
It launches.

00:05:23.615 --> 00:05:24.623
I think it does something like

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:27.754
25,000 downloads its first day

00:05:27.754 --> 00:05:30.872
and it sells to Facebook a year
later for a billion dollars,

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:34.590
so you could argue that
the best marketing decision

00:05:34.590 --> 00:05:37.736
that Instagram made was changing
from Burbn to Instagram.

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:39.400
Everything else was extra.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.916
Everything else was window dressing.

00:05:40.916 --> 00:05:43.733
The product was the marketing.

00:05:43.733 --> 00:05:45.739
- Most businesses are the same,

00:05:45.739 --> 00:05:49.014
whether you're selling energy products

00:05:49.014 --> 00:05:51.494
or whether any consumer products.

00:05:51.494 --> 00:05:55.748
If it's useful to people,
the rest is just business,

00:05:55.748 --> 00:05:58.685
so if you find something
or invent something

00:05:58.685 --> 00:06:01.685
that's really useful, at that point,

00:06:02.747 --> 00:06:05.719
it's really common,
whatever you're selling.

00:06:05.719 --> 00:06:09.123
The process is not that different

00:06:09.123 --> 00:06:11.220
and our process is maybe
a little bit different

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:14.051
because we really don't
have MBA type processing.

00:06:14.051 --> 00:06:16.545
We basically common sense.

00:06:16.545 --> 00:06:19.484
You know, we look at businesses
really in a simple way.

00:06:19.484 --> 00:06:22.199
You make good stuff and you sell it.

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:25.159
You know, instead of all
the high affluent words,

00:06:25.159 --> 00:06:28.929
which I really don't
understand like brand awareness

00:06:28.929 --> 00:06:33.845
and brand equity and all the
fancy terminologies people use

00:06:33.845 --> 00:06:35.618
that they don't have a product.

00:06:35.618 --> 00:06:39.785
So, what we did was we
simply stick to fundamentals,

00:06:41.458 --> 00:06:44.693
which is you make a really good product

00:06:44.693 --> 00:06:47.786
and 80% of marketing is
making a really good product

00:06:47.786 --> 00:06:52.326
and then you go out there
and if people like it,

00:06:52.326 --> 00:06:54.139
they agree with you, then they buy it.

00:06:54.139 --> 00:06:56.643
Actually, you'll find most of the products

00:06:56.643 --> 00:07:00.197
that they say are good are marginal.

00:07:00.197 --> 00:07:01.140
Right?

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.358
Most of the folks that
come up with products

00:07:03.358 --> 00:07:05.678
usually use some kind of a gimmick.

00:07:05.678 --> 00:07:08.774
In other words, gee, let's
make it pink if it's for women

00:07:08.774 --> 00:07:12.941
or let's make what's his name
famous person endorse it.

00:07:15.194 --> 00:07:18.165
Products themselves are marginal

00:07:18.165 --> 00:07:20.321
and then they say, "Well,
no, it's a good product."

00:07:20.321 --> 00:07:22.679
Well, my question is do you use it?

00:07:22.679 --> 00:07:24.154
If you're going to go
out there and sell it,

00:07:24.154 --> 00:07:25.614
do you use it yourself?

00:07:25.614 --> 00:07:27.071
Does your family use it?

00:07:27.071 --> 00:07:29.598
And if they don't, why are you selling it?

00:07:29.598 --> 00:07:32.498
You know, so really you'll find
most of the fellas that say,

00:07:32.498 --> 00:07:34.680
"We have great products,
but we didn't get success,"

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.569
is they really don't use the product.

00:07:36.569 --> 00:07:39.446
They think everybody
else should, but not us.

00:07:39.446 --> 00:07:41.974
We're superior to our own product,

00:07:41.974 --> 00:07:45.141
so there's a compromise amongst people

00:07:46.084 --> 00:07:48.402
about the quality of what they sell

00:07:48.402 --> 00:07:51.605
and quality's also misunderstood.

00:07:51.605 --> 00:07:53.432
In other words, quality starts with

00:07:53.432 --> 00:07:55.932
if a customer doesn't need it,

00:07:56.823 --> 00:08:00.990
if it doesn't provide something
that they're going to pay for.

00:08:01.933 --> 00:08:03.931
We have different technical terms.

00:08:03.931 --> 00:08:06.816
I mean, our technical terms are slam dunk.

00:08:06.816 --> 00:08:08.637
You know, everybody
understands what that is.

00:08:08.637 --> 00:08:11.736
You got a slam dunk product
or do you have a good product?

00:08:11.736 --> 00:08:15.769
For us, we get pitched
several products a week

00:08:15.769 --> 00:08:17.073
and if it's a good product,

00:08:17.073 --> 00:08:18.564
we don't go out there and sell it.

00:08:18.564 --> 00:08:20.440
It's got to be slam dunk.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.087
It's got to be where nobody will refuse it

00:08:24.087 --> 00:08:26.532
and then you have a hard time with it,

00:08:26.532 --> 00:08:30.699
so the flaw is really they
don't have good products.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:35.452
Definition of a good
product is it will do well.

00:08:35.452 --> 00:08:37.910
Now, there's some amount of execution,

00:08:37.910 --> 00:08:42.077
but I would say that if you
don't have a great product,

00:08:43.478 --> 00:08:46.172
any amount of execution is going to

00:08:46.172 --> 00:08:48.938
only buy you a little time.

00:08:48.938 --> 00:08:50.892
After that, everybody will figure out

00:08:50.892 --> 00:08:53.939
that this is not that good
and not buy it anymore.

